          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{g})
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebra"} differential graded algebra
            of elements dual to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
            encodes the Lie bracket on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
            of the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complex
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cohomology"} Lie algebra cohomology
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
          %p
            This generalizes to a notion of Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E-algebra"} L-∞-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroid"} Lie algebroid
            and generally an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
          %h2#GradingConvention Grading conventions
          %p This differential-graded subject is somewhat notorious for a plethora of equivalent but different conventions on gradings and signs.
          %p
            For the following we adopt the convention that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded vector space
            we write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo •
                          %mi V
                        %mtd
                          %mo :
                          %mo =
                          %mi Sym
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi k
                          %mo ⊕
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊕
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ⊕
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 0
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊕
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ⊕
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 0
                          %mo ⊕
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 0
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 0
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊕
                          %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\wedge^\bullet V &amp;:= Sym(V[1])
                  \\\
                  \&amp; = k \oplus (V_0) \oplus (V_1 \oplus V_0 \wedge V_0) \oplus
                  (V_2 \oplus V_1 \wedge V_0 \oplus V_0 \wedge V_0 \wedge V_0)
                  \\oplus \cdots
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+construction"} free
            graded-commutative algebra on the graded vector space obtained by shifting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %em up
            in degree by one.
          %p
            Here the elements in the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            A plain vector space, such as the dual
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^*
            of the vector space underlying a Lie algebra, is regarded as a
            = succeed "-graded" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge^\bullet \mathfrak{g}^*
            is the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmann+algebra"} Grassmann algebra
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^*
            are generators of degree 1.
          %h2#OfLieAlgebra Of Lie algebras
          %h3#DefForLieAlg Definition
          %p
            The
            %em Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{g})
            of a finite dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree
            graded-commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            whose underlying graded algebra is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmann+algebra"} Grassmann algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo =
                  %mi k
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\wedge^\bullet \mathfrak{g}^*
                  \=
                  k \oplus \mathfrak{g}^* \oplus (\mathfrak{g}^* \wedge \mathfrak{g}^* ) \oplus \cdots
          %p
            (with the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            (of degree +1) is defined on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^*
            as the dual of the Lie bracket
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d|_{\mathfrak{g}^*} := [-,-]^* : \mathfrak{g}^* \to \mathfrak{g}^* \wedge \mathfrak{g}^*,
          %p
            and extended uniquely as a graded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo •
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge^\bullet \mathfrak{g}^*
          %p
            That this differential indeed squares to 0,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi d
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \circ d = 0
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+identity"} Jacobi identity
          %p
            If we choose a dual basis
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{t^a\}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^*
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{C^a{}_{b c}\}
            be the structure constants of the Lie bracket in that basis, then the action of the differential on the basis generators is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mi c
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mi c
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d t^a = - \frac{1}{2} C^a{}_{b c} t^b \wedge t^c
                  \\,,
          %p where here and in the following a sum over repeated indices is implicit.
          %p This has a more or less evident generalization to infinite-dimensional Lie algebras.,
          %h3#PropertiesForLieAlg Properties
          %p
            One observes that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            a vector space, the graded-commutative dg-algebra structures on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge^\bullet \mathfrak{g}^*
            are precisely in bijection with Lie algebra structures on
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^*
            defines a skew-linear bracket and the condition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi d
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d^2 = 0
            holds if and only if that bracket satisfies the Jacobi identity.
          %p
            Moreover, morphisms if Lie algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} \to \mathfrak{h}
            are precisely in bijection with morphisms of dg-algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CE
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ←
                    %mi CE
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{g}) \leftarrow CE(\mathfrak{h})
            = succeed "-construction" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CE
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE
          %p
            Therefore, if we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi dgAlg
                    %mrow
                      %mi sf
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dgAlg_{sf,1}
            for the category whose objects are
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree dga
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LieAlg
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mrow
                      %mi CE
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi dgAlg
                    %mrow
                      %mi sf
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  LieAlg \stackrel{CE(-)}{\underset{\simeq}{\to}} (dgAlg_{sf,1})^{op}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where on the right we have the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
          %p This says that in a sense the Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra is just another way of looking at (finite dimensional) Lie algebras.
          %p
            There is an analogous statement not involving the dualization: Lie algebra structures on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            are also in bijection with the structure of a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+coalgebra"} differential graded coalgebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo •
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\vee^\bullet \mathfrak{g}, D)
            on the free graded-co-commutative coalgebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo •
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee^\bullet \mathfrak{g}
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
            of degree -1 squaring to 0.
          %p The relation between the differentials is simply dualization
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo •
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\vee^\bullet \mathfrak{g}, D) \leftrightarrow
                  (\wedge^\bullet \mathfrak{g}^* , d )
          %p
            where for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \in \wedge^\bullet \mathfrak{g}^*
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d \omega = \omega(D(-))
                  \\,.
          %h2#of_algebras
            Of
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
          %p
            The equivalence between Lie algebras and differential graded algebras/coalgebras discussed above suggests a grand generalization by simply generalizing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmann+algebra"} Grassmann algebra
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^*
            to the Grassmann algebra over a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded vector space
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            is a graded vector space, then a differential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            of degree -1 squaring to 0 on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo •
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee^\bullet \mathfrak{g}
            is precisely the same as equipping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            with the structure of an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
          %p
            Dually, this corresponds to a general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree dga
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  CE(\mathfrak{g}) := (\wedge^\bullet \mathfrak{g}^*, d = D^*)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This we may usefully think of as the Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra of the
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
          %p
            So
            %em every
            commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree dga
            (degreewise finite-dimensional) is the Chevaley-Eilenberg algebra of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
            of finite type.
          %p
            This means that many constructions involving
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+theory"} ∞-Lie theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sullivan+construction"} Sullivan construction
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
            may be interpreted in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of
            = succeed "-algebras." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
          %h2#OfLieAlgebroids Of Lie algebroids
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroid"} Lie algebroid
            given as
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\to X
            %li
              %p
                with anchor map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo :
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : E \to T X
            %li
              %p
                and bracket
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo ∧
                        %mi ℝ
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-] \;\colon\; \Gamma(E)\wedge_{\mathbb{R}} \Gamma(E) \to \Gamma(E)
          %p the corresponding Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∧
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  CE(\mathfrak{a}) := \left(\wedge^\bullet_{C^\infty(X)} \Gamma(E)^*, d\right)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where now the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X)
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (with values in the
            = succeed "s)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            is given by the formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Shuff
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi sgn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Shuff
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi sign
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (d\omega)(e_0, \cdots, e_n)
                  \=
                  \\sum_{\sigma \in Shuff(1,n)} sgn(\sigma) \rho(e_{\sigma(0)})(\omega(e_{\sigma(1)}, \cdots, e_{\sigma(n)}))
                  +
                  \\sum_{\sigma \in Shuff(2,n-1)} sign(\sigma)
                  \\omega([e_{\sigma(0)},e_{\sigma(1)}],e_{\sigma(2)}, \cdots, e_{\sigma(n)} )
                  \\,,
          %p
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \in \wedge^n_{C^\infty(X)} \Gamma(E)^*
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (e_i \in \Gamma(E))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Shuff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Shuff(p,q)
            denotes the set of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,q)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shuffle"} shuffle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sgn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sgn(\sigma)
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature"} signature
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ±
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in \{\pm 1\}
            of the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation"} permutation
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = *
            the point we have that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            is a Lie algebra and this definition reproduces the above definition of the CE-algebra of a Lie algebra (possibly up to an irrelevant global sign).
          %h2#of_lie_algebroids_2
            Of
            = succeed "-Lie" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#of_abelian_lie_algebras
            Of abelian Lie
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            The CE-algebra of the Lie algebra of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{u}(1)
            is the graded-commutative dg-algebra on a single generator in degree 1 with vanishing differential.
          %p
            More generally, the
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mi n
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b^n \mathfrak{u}(1)
            is the one whose CE algebra is the commutative dg-algebra with a single generator in degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            and vanishing differential.
          %h3#of_
            Of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{su}(2)
          %p
            The CE-algebra of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{su}(2)
            has three generators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y, z
            in degree one and differential
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d x_1 = x_2 \wedge x_3
          %p and cyclically.
          %h3#of_the_tangent_lie_algebroid_
            Of the tangent Lie algebroid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X
          %p
